From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Open Arc Authors <dev@openarc.invalid>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 23:08:57 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] openarc: embedder: profile deletion, clearing data, incognito
 teardown

Plan C11 (profiles: on-disk data directory per Profile, isolation of
cookies and storage). C6b maps each Open Arc Profile to a ProfileManager
profile at <user data dir>/<Profile UUID>, its primary off-the-record
profile for incognito. This commit fills the three parts the bridge
left as stubs:

- OACApplication.deleteProfile: refuses while a tab of the profile
  (regular or off the record) is open. Otherwise it does what Chrome's
  DeleteProfileHelper does minus its Browser-window steps (there are no
  Browser windows; the shell, not Chrome, picks profiles): schedules and
  marks the directory for deletion (recorded in Local State, so a crash
  removes the files at the next start; the profile turns ephemeral, so
  ProfileManager removes it when it unloads it), cancels downloads, wipes
  the browsing data (Profile::Wipe), destroys the off-the-record profile,
  removes the attributes entry and drops the bridge's keep-alive. Once
  Chrome has destroyed the Profile (or after 30 s) it removes the
  directory and its cache directory itself and only then calls back, so
  the caller knows the data is gone.
- OACProfile.clearBrowsingData: Chrome's BrowsingDataRemover with
  //chrome's delegate. Cookies also clear the HTTP auth cache; site
  storage is Chrome's "site data" without cookies (DOM storage, service
  workers, cache storage, media licenses, site usage); history, cache,
  downloads list, passwords, autofill and content settings map to
  Chrome's data types.
- Incognito: when the last off-the-record tab of a profile closes, the
  bridge destroys the primary OTR profile (ProfileDestroyer, after its
  renderers exit) and tells the application delegate. Upstream Chrome
  does this when the last incognito Browser closes, which never happens
  here. A new incognito tab while the old context is still being torn
  down destroys it at once, so it always starts empty.
